-- title:
--   Existence of a level-N annulus datum satisfying all its laws
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $q$, a valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, an integer $N \ge 1$, an algebraically closed field $k$ of characteristic $q$, a ring map $\mathrm{red} : A \to k$, modular polynomial data `data` for $q$ satisfying the Kronecker congruence `hKr`, and integrality hypotheses `hα`, `hβ` for the two degeneracy embeddings of the level-$N$ function field into the level-$Nq$ one over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$. Let $P$ be a place specialisation of these data, assume $q \nmid N$, let $W$ be a finite set of places of $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldC}\,k\,N$ whose members are exactly the supersingular places $\mathrm{ssPlaces}\,q\,N\,k$, and let $R$ be a prolongation tuple for $P$ which is a model (the two divisor laws and the two cusp laws), and satisfies the regularity law and the node-value law at $W$, the fixed-order law, and the value-integrality law at each $w \in W$. Then there is a level-$N$ annulus datum $\mathrm{dat}$ over $W$ — consisting of intermediate fields $K_w \subseteq \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, node coordinates $(x_w, y_w)$ over $K_w$ at each $w \in W$, widths $e_w \in \mathbb{N}$, a depth function $\delta$ on places of the level-$Nq$ field over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, uniformisers $\pi_w, \pi'_w$, correction divisors $R_w, R'_w$, and units $\bar u_0(w), \lambda_w, \mu_w$ of $k$ — with the following properties. For $w \in W$: $1 \le e_w$ and $e_w = \mathrm{placeWidthChar}\,q\,N\,w$. For every place $V$ of the level-$Nq$ field with $P.\mathrm{reduceFst}\,V = w$ which is strict on neither side: $0 < \delta(V) < e_w$ and $\mathrm{yDepth}(V)^{\mathrm{den}\,\delta(V)} = v_A(q)^{\mathrm{num}\,\delta(V)}$ in the value group of $A$, where $\mathrm{yDepth}(V) = v_A(V(y_w))$. The function $\delta$ is invariant under the arithmetic Galois action of every $\sigma$ in the inertia subgroup of $A$ over $\mathbb{Q}$; and whenever $e_w \ge 2$ there is such a $V$ above $w$, fixed by that action of every inertia element, with $\delta(V) = 1$. For $w \in W$ the divisor $(w) + R_w$ is the order divisor of $\pi_w$, with $R_w$ vanishing on $W$ and $\deg R_w = -1$, and likewise $(\varphi w) + R'_w$ is the order divisor of $\pi'_w$ for $\varphi = \mathrm{arithFrobC}\,q\,k\,N$. Every inertia element fixes $K_w$ pointwise for $w \in W$, and each $K_w$ is finite over $\mathbb{Q}$. Finally there exist elements $\varpi_w$ and $\varepsilon_w$ of the coefficient ring $A \cap K_w$, natural numbers $e^K_w$, and elements $u_w$ of the node ring $R.\mathrm{nodeIntegersOver}\,(K_w)\,w$ such that, for $w \in W$: the kernel of the reduction $A \cap K_w \to k$ is the ideal generated by $\varpi_w$; $1 \le e^K_w$; $\varepsilon_w$ is a unit reducing to $1$, and $q = \varpi_w^{e^K_w} \varepsilon_w$; $u_w$ is a unit with $x_w y_w = \mathrm{nodeConst}(\varpi_w)^{e_w e^K_w} u_w$; the ideal generated by $\mathrm{nodeConst}(\varpi_w), x_w, y_w$ is maximal and is the only maximal ideal of the node ring; the ideals generated by $\mathrm{nodeConst}(\varpi_w), x_w$ and by $\mathrm{nodeConst}(\varpi_w), y_w$ are prime, with $y_w$ outside the former and $x_w$ outside the latter; the node ring is Noetherian, and for every element $g$ of it some $\mathrm{nodeConst}(o)$, $o \in A \cap K_w$, makes $g - \mathrm{nodeConst}(o)$ a non-unit; and $w$ takes the value $\bar u_0(w)$ at the first residue of $u_w$ and the value $\lambda_w$ at the first residue of $y_w$ divided by $\pi_w$, while $\varphi w$ takes the value $\mu_w$ at the second residue of $x_w$ divided by $\pi'_w$.
--
--   This is the existence statement for the level-$N$ annulus datum attached to the supersingular points of the reduction of $X_0(N)$ at $q$: it produces, in one package, the local crossing coordinates at each supersingular node, their widths, the inertia-invariant depth function on the places above them, local uniformisers with correction divisors, and the local presentation of the node rings. It makes non-vacuous the statements that take these laws as hypotheses, and is used in the deduction of the Picard-group results [`ModularCurve.PlaceSpecialization.exists_inertiaFixedSupport_degZero_pic0Mk_eq_of_isModel`](thm.html#ModularCurve.PlaceSpecialization.exists_inertiaFixedSupport_degZero_pic0Mk_eq_of_isModel) and [`ModularCurve.PlaceSpecialization.exists_isGoodDiv_pic0Mk_eq_of_comp_eq_zero_of_depthCompLaw_depthValueLaw_repOfInvariant_of_isModel`](thm.html#ModularCurve.PlaceSpecialization.exists_isGoodDiv_pic0Mk_eq_of_comp_eq_zero_of_depthCompLaw_depthValueLaw_repOfInvariant_of_isModel).
